 # Shard count, 1..256. Shards outnumber workers so migration stays fine-grained.
 # shards 16
 
-# SORT's BY/GET DEREFERENCE IS PLACEMENT-DEPENDENT, and has no knob of its own.
-# `SORT key BY weight_* GET data_*` names one key per ELEMENT of `key`, and those names are only
-# known after the element has been read on its owner -- too late for the router to have told the
-# other owners about them. A shard is touched only by its owning executor, so the dereference is
-# admitted exactly when that cannot be violated: when the placement gives ONE executor thread, which
-# therefore owns every shard. With more than one executor a '*'-bearing BY or GET is refused with
-#   ERR BY option of SORT denied when keys formed by the pattern may be owned by another executor
-# (and the GET spelling), the same shape the reference server uses to refuse the same patterns in
-# cluster mode. Everything that dereferences nothing -- BY with a pattern that has no '*' (which
-# suppresses ordering), GET #, a GET pattern with no '*', LIMIT/ALPHA/ASC/DESC/STORE -- works in
-# every placement. See NOTES-SORT.md for the design and the options that were rejected.
+# SORT BY/GET dereference has no placement knob. `SORT key BY weight_* GET data_*` first snapshots
+# the source on its owner, expands one concrete key per element, then gathers those keys from their
+# ordinary shard owners. Source and derived reads share one snapshot and wall-clock cut; no executor
+# touches another executor's store. `SORT ... STORE` sends the completed projection through the
+# ordinary destination-owner path. Globless BY still suppresses ordering, globless GET returns NULL,
+# and GET # returns the source element without a derived lookup. See NOTES-SORTXSHARD.md.
 
