Implemented and committed on `t-sortxshard`.

- SORT now snapshots the source, expands concrete BY/GET keys, gathers them from their shard owners, then performs ordering/projection once.
- Source and gathered reads share one snapshot and wall-clock cut.
- `SORT ... STORE` retains the existing destination-owner hop.
- EXEC/MULTI staging supports dynamically discovered owners.
- State lives in the scatter arena’s cold tail; `Op` and `Client` were unchanged.
- `sort_deref_local`, `kSortByDenied`, `kSortGetDenied`, and the old negative-control path were deleted.
- MVCC resolver and atomic storage-engine files were untouched; only task key/hazard enumeration was taught about dynamic SORT requests.
- Findings and design are recorded in [NOTES-SORTXSHARD.md](/home/user/Projects/tomokv-cpp-sortxshard/NOTES-SORTXSHARD.md).

Validation definitions explicitly require `--shards 16 --ratio 6:2 --enable-debug-command yes`, in both atomic modes. They walk candidate names with `DEBUG SHARD`, observe all 16 shards, and fail loudly unless concrete BY and GET keys—and the STORE destination—are on the executor opposite the source. The differential SORT leg independently proves generated-command cross-owner geometry.

Per your lane rules, I did not build, run `make`, start a server, or execute any tests. Static symbol and diff checks passed. Five incremental commits were created; the only remaining worktree entries are the pre-existing untracked `BRIEF.md` and `codex.log`.
